WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.932
Now we move the same Glucose condition from ON into WHERE.

00:00:03.932 --> 00:00:09.817
The LEFT JOIN first creates a result containing Ana's lab rows, Ben's Cholesterol row,

00:00:09.817 --> 00:00:12.572
and Cara with NULL lab values.

00:00:12.572 --> 00:00:16.487
At that moment, the left join has preserved every patient.

00:00:16.487 --> 00:00:22.082
Then the WHERE condition runs and keeps only rows whose test name equals Glucose.

00:00:22.082 --> 00:00:27.727
Ana's Glucose row survives. Ana's Cholesterol row is removed.

00:00:27.727 --> 00:00:28.062
Ben's Cholesterol row is removed.

00:00:28.062 --> 00:00:33.947
Cara's NULL value does not equal Glucose, so Cara is also removed.

00:00:33.947 --> 00:00:39.672
This is why a LEFT JOIN can behave like a matched-only result when a right-side condition is placed

00:00:39.672 --> 00:00:45.027
in WHERE. The JOIN keyword did not change, but the final population did.

00:00:45.027 --> 00:00:51.022
Run this query and compare the result with the previous slide.

00:00:51.022 --> 00:00:53.272
The difference is not cosmetic.

00:00:53.272 --> 00:00:55.952
It changes the meaning of the analysis.

00:00:55.952 --> 00:01:02.202
If the requirement says every patient must remain, this WHERE version is not equivalent to the previous ON

00:01:02.202 --> 00:01:03.094
version.
